WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.367
-Welcome back.
-Love you, Austin!

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:04.667
[ Laughter ]
-It is great to see you again.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:07.534
Congrats on every single thing
that's happening.

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:08.801
I gotta talk about this.

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:12.267
You got nominated for a SAG,
BAFTA, and an Oscar.

00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:13.834
Come on.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.834 --> 00:00:15.767
You won the Golden Globe.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:18.200
I called it before it came out,
I go, "Dude, it was

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.634
the best performance of the year
for me, that I've seen."

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:22.567
You're amazing in this.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:25.434
-How does it all feel
to get all this stuff and...?

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:27.734
-I mean, it feels --
it feels like a dream.

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:30.000
It really -- I've been doing
this for a long time.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.367
And so, a night like that,
it was so special.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.801
-The Golden Globes?
-The Golden Globes.

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.100
-Yeah.
-You know, to get to share that

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.400
with my sister and with
Priscilla and Lisa Marie.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.067
It was just so special,
you know.

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.334
-I saw that you brought
your sister to the thing.

00:00:46.334 --> 00:00:48.033
That's very cool.
Did she have a good time?

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:49.734
-She was the life of the party.
-Was she?

00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:51.501
-Yeah.
She would just come up to me

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:54.734
and she'd say, like, "That guy
looks just like James Marsden."

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:56.234
And she was talking to him
for 20 minutes.

00:00:56.234 --> 00:00:58.067
I said, "That is James Marsden,
Ashley."

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.200
[ Laughter ]
-She loves James Marsden.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.167
-She loves James Marsden.
-That's fantastic.

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.100
-She's got a husband,
but he was a gentleman to her.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.901
He was fantastic.
-It's James Marsden.

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:08.868
-It's James Marsden.
-But when you win and you get up

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:12.334
and you start talking, do you
know what you're gonna say?

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:14.634
Do you have a speech?
-Well, what they didn't tell me

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:17.200
is that you get up there
in front of everybody

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.200
you've ever admired,
on live television,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.000
and you get up on stage,
and there's a screen in the back

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.767
that says "30 seconds,"
and it's counting down.

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:28.501
And then, after 30 seconds,
it starts flashing in red,

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:30.367
"Wrap it up, wrap it up."

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:33.667
And so, you're up there,
completely blacked out,

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:36.934
trying to just string together
some comprehensible sentences.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:38.200
And I walked off stage
just thinking,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.434
"I hope that I said something."

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:42.167
-No, you were great.
You were great.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:43.901
But, yeah, all you're seeing
is, "Wrap it up, wrap it up."

00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:46.267
"Uh...wrap it up?"
-Yeah, it was so special.

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.367
-You thanked Denzel Washington.
-Yeah.

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.767
-What was the connection
with Denzel and you?

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:53.934
-Well, the moment that changed
my whole career,

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:55.300
'cause I've been doing this
since I was 12,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.400
but it was doing a play
with Denzel,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.634
"The Iceman Cometh,"
here in New York.

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:01.067
-Yeah.
-And it just --

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.901
it shifted my whole career
in many ways.

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:06.434
And he was so kind to me.

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:09.501
And being in that rehearsal room
with him, I mean, he's --

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:11.701
He will go down in history
as one of the greatest actors

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.367
to ever live.
-Yeah.

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:16.968
-And he was so generous to me.

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:18.467
-Did you get nervous meeting him
for the first time?

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:19.501
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.067
I mean,
it's like meeting royalty.

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:22.634
And it's like meeting, you know,

00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:24.834
your hero from "Training Day"
at the same time.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:26.968
[ Laughter ]
So, it's really intense.

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:29.300
-It's everything, yeah.
-And so, I really --

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.734
I was so scared.
So, I memorized the entire --

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:34.267
It's a four-hour play.
I memorized the entire thing

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:36.400
before the first table read,
so that way,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.400
I could sit there with him.
I sat right next to him,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.968
and I could sit there
and not look at the script

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:42.501
the whole time.
I just came out there, just --

00:02:42.501 --> 00:02:44.934
I knew I had to just
give it everything I had.

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:47.400
So, that --
yeah, it changed my life.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.934
But he was so kind.
-And to show him that

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:52.300
you meant business in your work.
-Yeah, and then he --

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.868
I mean, I still haven't
talked to him since,

00:02:53.868 --> 00:02:57.200
but he called Baz
when I was screentesting

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.634
for the role
and kind of vouched for me.

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:02.634
So I feel like I owe him a lot.

00:03:02.634 --> 00:03:05.534
-Yeah, I heard that you were
actually practicing

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:08.601
before you even auditioned
for the role of Elvis.

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:11.033
-Well, I mean, I heard Baz
was making the movie,

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:12.567
and I didn't even
have an audition.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:16.067
But the day that I heard
he was making it, I --

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:18.100
Some delusional thought
came over my mind

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.300
that I just need to give this
everything that I have.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.634
And so, I called a dialect coach
and a singing coach

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:27.234
and a movement coach,
and I just started working

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:28.334
like I had the job.

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:29.734
-Before you even
got to audition?

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:32.501
-Before I even met Baz.
And then a couple months later,

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:34.701
then we met, and I was already
down that road.

00:03:34.701 --> 00:03:37.300
So that's how I ended up --
-When you see the movie,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.467
you just can't even believe it.

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:40.501
It's you singing.
It's you moving.

00:03:40.501 --> 00:03:42.267
It's you --
You get that feeling,

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:44.701
that excitement, that energy.
You're like, "Oh, that's what

00:03:44.701 --> 00:03:46.968
it must've been like
when Elvis first --

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:49.267
You know, when it happened.
And then you took it

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.167
to the next level,
and I'm so happy you're getting

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:53.133
nominated for all this stuff.

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:55.534
Obviously, everyone's shocked
and saddened

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:59.601
by Lisa Marie Presley
passing away so suddenly.

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:01.968
But you got to hang with her.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:03.400
She got to see your work.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.968
You got to actually --
You were in the room with her.

00:04:05.968 --> 00:04:07.801
-Yeah.
-That must have been --

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:11.200
What was that like
in the early stages of the movie

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.167
and filming
and getting to know her?

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:18.634
-I first met Lisa when we
screened the film at Graceland.

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.367
-Yeah.
-And I've never had

00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:24.868
an experience where
I met somebody

00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:29.734
and felt an immediate depth
of relationship to them.

00:04:29.734 --> 00:04:31.968
And she just --
And she's also --

00:04:31.968 --> 00:04:35.501
She was a person who didn't
really have anything to prove

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:38.000
and wouldn't
open up to everybody,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.234
and she just opened up to me,
and we got so close so fast.

00:04:43.234 --> 00:04:46.167
So, yeah, it's just devastating.
And it's --

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:49.300
It's weird in moments like this
because it's so bittersweet.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.300
You just -- I mean, so much
great stuff is happening,

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.467
but it's really --

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.834
I'm just, you know,
devastated for her family.

00:04:56.834 --> 00:04:58.501
-Sure.
-And for her to not be here

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.334
celebrating with us, you know?

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:01.801
-I'm happy
that she got to see you.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.367
-Yeah.
And, I mean, just to get

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:04.634
to be with her at
the Golden Globes, like,

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:06.501
I'll never forget that
for the rest of my life.

00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:09.133
-To give you the support
and to give you the thumbs-up.

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:11.567
Was it Lisa Marie
or was it Priscilla

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.367
that showed you Elvis' slippers?

00:05:13.367 --> 00:05:14.501
-It was Lisa Marie.

00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:15.868
-Yeah, I remember
you told me that.

00:05:15.868 --> 00:05:18.534
And I was like -- I got chills.
-I get goose bumps.

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:20.801
I mean, that was after
we screened the film.

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:24.934
And the bedroom -- you know,
his bedroom is a place

00:05:24.934 --> 00:05:26.534
that most people
never get to see.

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:28.901
And she said, you know,
"I want to show you something."

00:05:28.901 --> 00:05:30.968
And she took me up there.
And it was --

00:05:30.968 --> 00:05:33.734
You know, moments like that,
we just sat in his bedroom,

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:36.000
and up there, he's not Elvis.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.801
He was just dad, you know?
-Yeah.

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:39.968
-And so, to hear those stories,

00:05:39.968 --> 00:05:41.901
I truly
will treasure that forever.

00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:44.300
-Did you get
to keep anything from the set?

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.667
-Yeah, yeah.
I kept a number of things.

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:50.200
But I kept, you know,
the black leather from '68.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.968
-No.
-I kept that.

00:05:51.968 --> 00:05:53.567
-They let you do that?
-Yeah.

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:55.200
I mean, it's in a box right now,
but I need to, like,

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.434
put it on a mannequin
or something.

00:05:56.434 --> 00:05:57.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.901 --> 00:06:00.567
-You have it. Yeah, as soon as
you walk into your house.

00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:03.000
-Eventually I'll do something.
-Well, that's so cool.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.100
-So, I have that.
And then when I wrapped,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.934
Baz gave me the back of the
"Unchained Melody" jumpsuit.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.801
You know the medallion
on the back of that?

00:06:10.801 --> 00:06:13.400
So, I have that framed at home.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.901
You were saying, too,
I read in an interview,

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:19.133
that you talked to Rami Malek
before you did this.

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:22.501
And you go, "You got any advice
on how to play such an icon?"

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:23.901
And he gave you
some great advice.

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:27.501
-Yeah. So, we had the same
movement coach, Polly Bennett,

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:30.200
who he worked with
on "Bohemian Rhapsody."

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.634
-Oh, my gosh.
-She's just the best.

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:36.567
And so she said, you know,
"Nobody knows what

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:38.300
you're going through
like Rami does,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.701
so I'll connect you to him."
And so she connected us.

00:06:40.701 --> 00:06:43.834
And I was really nervous,

00:06:43.834 --> 00:06:45.434
especially for
the live performances

00:06:45.434 --> 00:06:49.400
because I was never a singer,
I was never a dancer.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.934
I'm always a wallflower
at a party.

00:06:50.934 --> 00:06:52.300
I'm very shy.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.801
So, I was naturally perfect
to play Elvis.

00:06:54.801 --> 00:06:56.434
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly, oh, my God.

00:06:56.434 --> 00:06:57.667
You have all those things
going for you.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.334
-I had all that going for me.
-"He's perfect!

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:01.167
Except he doesn't do that."
-Yeah.

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:03.501
So, but I talked to Rami,
and he said,

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:07.067
"You know, those days
seem like the most daunting,

00:07:07.067 --> 00:07:08.801
but when you get out there
in front of the audience

00:07:08.801 --> 00:07:11.067
and you feel a genuine rapport

00:07:11.067 --> 00:07:14.400
and you see genuine emotion
on these people's faces,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.067
something ends up happening
that you can never expect."

00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:19.734
And those days ended up becoming
my favorite days on set

00:07:19.734 --> 00:07:22.667
and probably that I'll ever have
'cause it's the closest thing

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:24.834
to what Elvis himself
experienced, you know?

00:07:24.834 --> 00:07:26.434
-Yeah.
Do you like that when you

00:07:26.434 --> 00:07:28.734
go into a role,
to challenge yourself?

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:31.067
I mean, you do "Iceman Cometh"
with Denzel,

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:34.234
you're gonna play Elvis Presley
and you don't sing or dance.

00:07:34.234 --> 00:07:37.801
-Yeah. I think that's what it's
all kind of about is, like,

00:07:37.801 --> 00:07:41.534
exploring bits of yourself
that you may not in real life.

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:44.434
You know?
-Yeah.

00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:46.934
-And also using fear
as a compass.

00:07:46.934 --> 00:07:48.334
You know, I often say that.

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:52.234
Because then it expands
your comfort zone.

00:07:52.234 --> 00:07:53.901
And I was such a repressed kid.

00:07:53.901 --> 00:07:55.934
Like, I wouldn't order food
for myself at a restaurant.

00:07:55.934 --> 00:07:59.868
I wouldn't -- I was so shy.
So, it's all this kind of

00:07:59.868 --> 00:08:03.601
personal thing of getting to
explore these bits of yourself

00:08:03.601 --> 00:08:05.234
that you never do otherwise.

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:06.701
-You hit a home run with this,
buddy.

00:08:06.701 --> 00:08:08.634
"Elvis" is nominated
for eight Academy Awards,

00:08:08.634 --> 00:08:09.968
including Best Picture.

00:08:09.968 --> 00:08:12.834
You're nominated for Best Actor.
Congratulations, buddy.

00:08:12.834 --> 00:08:15.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:15.667 --> 00:08:17.434
Awesome.

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:19.534
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:19.534 --> 00:08:21.868
Here is Austin Butler
in "Elvis."

00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:24.200
Take a look at this.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:28.267
-♪ I'm evil, evil,
evil as can be ♪

00:08:28.267 --> 00:08:31.467
♪ So don't mess around,
don't mess around with me ♪

00:08:31.467 --> 00:08:32.601
♪♪

00:08:32.601 --> 00:08:34.567
♪ I'm evil ♪

00:08:34.567 --> 00:08:37.133
♪ And I'm evil ♪

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:39.234
♪ Evil ♪
[ Camera shutter clicks ]

00:08:39.234 --> 00:08:41.567
♪ Evil ♪

00:08:41.567 --> 00:08:44.834
-Now would be a good time
to -- to get back into the car.

00:08:44.834 --> 00:08:47.033
-♪ I'm evil ♪

00:08:47.033 --> 00:08:50.968
♪ Evil ♪
♪ Evil ♪

00:08:50.968 --> 00:08:53.567
[ Audio distorting ]

00:08:53.567 --> 00:08:56.267
[ Crowd cheering ]

00:08:56.267 --> 00:08:57.501
[ Ominous music plays ]

00:08:57.501 --> 00:09:01.667
♪ I'm evil ♪

00:09:01.667 --> 00:09:03.467
-Get that boy off that stage!

00:09:03.467 --> 00:09:06.267
♪♪

00:09:06.267 --> 00:09:07.968
-That's Austin Butler
right there.

00:09:07.968 --> 00:09:09.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:09.767 --> 00:09:12.067
Austin Butler, "Elvis,"
is in theaters

00:09:12.067 --> 00:09:14.601
and streaming now on HBO Max.

00:09:14.601 --> 00:09:16.534
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:09:16.534 --> 00:09:18.133
Stick around, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

